{"lines": ["On the fifteenth of November, 1966, two young couples, Roger and Linda Scarberry and Steve and Mary Mallette, told police they saw a large dark creature with glowing red eyes near the old TNT area outside Point Pleasant, West Virginia.", "The TNT area was a World War Two munitions plant. They said they drove away at a hundred miles an hour, and that the thing kept pace.", "The next morning, the Point Pleasant Register printed it.", "Now look at the name. The word Mothman most likely came from the Batman TV show, which was popular then, and had a villain called the Killer Moth. Local papers ran with it.", "Nobody who was there called it that first.", "Sheriff George Johnson thought it was an unusually large green heron. A witness, Newell Partridge, described eyes like bicycle reflectors, a buzzing television, and a missing dog named Bandit.", "A wildlife biologist at West Virginia University, Robert Smith, said it fit a sandhill crane, with a seven foot wingspan and reddish skin around the eyes. Just a bird far outside its migration range.", "Rule four. Be careful with the name. Once a thing has a name, memory fills in the rest."], "words": [["On", 0.0, 0.12], ["the", 0.12, 0.18], ["fifteenth", 0.18, 0.76], ["of", 0.76, 0.81], ["November,", 0.81, 1.22], ["1966,", 1.6, 2.44], ["two", 2.6, 3.36], ["young", 3.36, 3.6], ["couples,", 3.6, 3.98], ["Roger", 4.34, 4.46], ["and", 4.46, 4.9], ["Linda", 4.9, 5.12], ["Scarberry", 5.12, 5.68], ["and", 5.68, 5.96], ["Steve", 5.96, 6.26], ["and", 6.26, 6.62], ["Mary", 6.62, 6.82], ["Mallette,", 6.82, 7.76], ["told", 7.76, 7.9], ["police", 7.9, 8.18], ["they", 8.18, 8.62], ["saw", 8.62, 8.84], ["a", 8.84, 9.02], ["large", 9.02, 9.22], ["dark", 9.22, 9.74], ["creature", 9.74, 10.26], ["with", 10.26, 10.52], ["glowing", 10.52, 10.76], ["red", 10.76, 11.14], ["eyes", 11.14, 11.56], ["near", 11.56, 11.92], ["the", 11.92, 12.06], ["old", 12.06, 12.32], ["TNT", 12.32, 12.6], ["area", 12.6, 13.42], ["outside", 13.42, 13.78], ["